## 1. Scope

This reference covers filament wound epoxy glass tubes, as distinct from rolled and cured tube. Filament winding puts continuous fibre along a controlled path, which is why the process is chosen when mechanical load and insulation have to be met in the same part.

## 2. Construction

- Continuous glass fibre wound on a mandrel at a controlled angle and impregnated with epoxy resin.
- Fibre angle sets the balance between axial and hoop strength, so the winding pattern is part of the specification.
- Mandrel is removed after cure, and the tube is then machined to final length and features.
- Wall thickness is built by winding passes rather than by laminating sheet.

## 3. Typical specification range

| Parameter | Typical value | Notes |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Process | Filament winding on a mandrel, epoxy matrix | Distinct from rolled tube in fibre continuity and directional strength. |
| Mechanical properties | Flexural and tensile per ASTM D790 and ASTM D638 | Winding angle determines how the tube behaves in each direction. |
| Electrical properties | Dielectric strength per ASTM D149, insulation resistance per ASTM D257 | Declared for the tube build, not for a generic laminate. |
| Grades | G10 and G11 epoxy glass types | The machining characteristics of the two differ. |
