## 4. Selection and sizing

- Whether the load on the tube is mainly axial or hoop, since that sets the winding angle.
- Wall thickness against the load case, because a thin wall wound tube is where cracking starts.
- The machining operations required afterwards, since machining cuts the continuous fibre.
- Concentricity and straightness tolerance needed for the assembly.
- Whether the part is a standard tube or a special size, since mandrel availability drives lead time.

## 5. Standards commonly specified

- ASTM D790, flexural properties of reinforced plastics.
- ASTM D638, tensile properties of plastics.
- ASTM D149, dielectric breakdown voltage of solid insulating materials.
- ASTM D257, DC resistance or conductance of insulating materials.
- NEMA LI 1 and IEC 60893 series, laminated and composite insulation grades.
- IEC 60893-3-2, requirements for rigid laminated sheets based on epoxy resins.

Standards are listed as those normally cited for this component class. They describe the requirement, not a claim of certification. Test evidence applicable to a specific order is confirmed per purchase order.

## 6. Handling and installation

- Support thin wall tubes along their length during machining. A tube held at the ends and cut in the middle is where delamination starts.
- Use sharp tooling and control the feed so heat does not build up at the cut.
- Deburr machined ends and remove dust before the part enters an electrical assembly.
- Store horizontally and supported, since a long thin wall tube sags under its own weight.

## 7. Failure modes and mitigation

| Failure mode | Likely cause | Mitigation |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Thin wall cracking during machining | Insufficient support and a cut that loads the wall between the supports | Support along the length and reduce the cut depth per pass. |
| Interlaminar delamination | Winding or cure not controlled for the tube build | Confirm the build against the specification and check at incoming. |
| Machining burrs in the assembly | Tooling life or feed speed not controlled | Sharpen tooling on a schedule and inspect machined ends. |
| Tube out of tolerance in concentricity | Mandrel or winding tension variation, or distortion after cure | Set the concentricity tolerance against requirement and verify at incoming. |

## 8. Typical applications

- Switchgear and breaker components where insulation and mechanical load combine.
- Medical equipment and industrial machinery requiring a strong insulating tube.
- Aerospace and cryogenic equipment where a specific stiffness and thermal profile is needed.
- Fuse and interrupter bodies requiring a structural tube.

